# W00-13 — template. Copy per machine; real .env files are gitignored.
# `npm run check:env` validates the deployment matrix before anything boots.

APP_ENV=local

# One database per environment. Local values default in compose.yml.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://concierge:concierge@localhost:54329/concierge_local

# W00-13 — the three-environment gate. `npm run check:env` reads these three and
# refuses if staging and prod share a database, or if either reuses the local one.
# They are deployment-time values, not runtime ones: the running process only ever
# sees its own DATABASE_URL.
# LOCAL_DATABASE_URL=postgres://concierge:concierge@localhost:54329/concierge_local

# The Phase B deployment matrix (checked, never assumed):
# STAGING_DATABASE_URL=postgres://...neon-or-supabase-staging...
# PROD_DATABASE_URL=postgres://...neon-or-supabase-prod...

LOG_LEVEL=debug
SENTRY_DSN=

APP_NAME=ai-guest-concierge

# S02-01 — Neon Auth (managed Better Auth) JWT verification. The base URL is
# also the default issuer/audience; override explicitly if the branch's tokens
# carry different values. JWKS is the branch endpoint probed in W00.
# NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL=https://<branch>.neonauth.<region>.aws.neon.tech/<db>/auth
# NEON_AUTH_JWKS_URL=https://<branch>.neonauth.<region>.aws.neon.tech/<db>/auth/.well-known/jwks.json
# NEON_AUTH_ISSUER=
# NEON_AUTH_AUDIENCE=


# S04-01 — the staff review screen (web/) is a Next.js app that consumes the
# JSON surface above through one proxy route. It runs as its own npm project:
#   npm run web:install && npm run web:dev      # or web:build
# REVIEW_API_URL is the ONLY address the screen calls, and only from the
# Next server (never from the browser): the staff token stays in an HttpOnly
# cookie and the proxy attaches it.
REVIEW_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787

# S07-01 — the PUBLIC origin of the guest chat (`/c/:slug`), used to build the
# URL a QR code encodes and the URL the embed snippet frames. Unset is allowed:
# the routes fall back to the request's own origin, which is right for local dev
# and preview hosts. It must be set in production — a QR encoded with an
# internal hostname scans to nothing, and a printed card outlives the deploy
# that produced it. Bare origin only, no path.
# GUEST_PUBLIC_URL=https://chat.example.com

# S04-02 / DEC-3 — embedding provider for chunking and publishing.
# DEC-3 (operator, 2026-10-25): OpenAI `text-embedding-3-small` over plain
# `fetch`; no SDK. The model must emit 1536 dimensions (D3 locks the column);
# anything else fails in assertEmbeddingDimension before it reaches Postgres.
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY=sk-...
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# EMBEDDING_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
# EMBEDDING_BATCH_SIZE=16

# --- S05-01 / DEC-2: LLM (Google Gemini) -----------------------------------
# Both model ids are REQUIRED and must be pinned: a `-latest` alias is refused
# by loadLlmConfig, because the same alias is a different model in six months.
# Recommended pair: gemini-2.5-flash (primary) / gemini-2.5-flash-lite (fallback).
# LLM_API_KEY is required outside APP_ENV=local; CI uses a stub provider.
# LLM_PRIMARY_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# LLM_FALLBACK_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-lite
# LLM_API_KEY=AIza...
# LLM_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta
# LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
# LLM_RETRIES=2

# S06-01 — the public guest chat signs its anonymous identity cookie with this.
# Required (>= 32 chars) wherever /c/:slug is served: a default would be a
# published signing key, so an unset value makes the guest routes answer 503
# with a reason instead of handing out forgeable cookies.
# GUEST_COOKIE_SECRET=<openssl rand -base64 32>


# --- S07-04: abuse + cost protection ---------------------------------------
# Two unrelated controls that both exist because /c/:slug is public.
#
# 1. Rate limits are NOT configurable here. They are constants in
#    src/security/ratelimit.ts (20/min per hashed IP, 10/min per conversation,
#    600/hour per property). A ceiling a property can raise is not a ceiling.
#
# 2. The bot check (Cloudflare Turnstile, or any vendor behind the port in
#    src/security/botcheck.ts). TURNSTILE_SECRET is required outside
#    APP_ENV=local: a host that serves real guests must not run without the
#    control it was configured to use.
#    W00-18 / DEC-9: enabling this sends guest IPs and browser metadata to a
#    third-party processor, so it must appear in the privacy notice and be
#    covered by a DPA BEFORE production. Until then, staging runs with it.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET=<from the Cloudflare dashboard>
# TURNSTILE_TIMEOUT_MS=3000
#
# The site key is public by design and is read by the browser bundle (web/),
# not by the API. Absent ⇒ no widget renders and the server is not enforcing.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=<from the Cloudflare dashboard>

# S07-05 — the outage path, made testable instead of hypothetical.
# `none` (default) | `once` (the next generation fails, then normal) |
# `always` (every generation fails). Each failure is a real AiUnavailableError,
# so the guest gets the catalogue "a person will reply" string, a service request
# is captured, and ai_runs/message_failed record the outage.
# Refused outright when APP_ENV=prod: a host that can be made to fail its AI by
# an environment variable is a host where one copied file is an outage.
# AI_FAIL_MODE=none

# --- S09-05: escalation email (Resend) --------------------------------------
# How a request reaches a human who does not have the staff console open. The
# row is queued inside the request's own transaction, so "the request exists"
# and "somebody will be told" are one atomic fact; a provider outage produces a
# retrying row, not a silence.
#
# EMAIL_FROM is required in EVERY environment and has no default: a default
# sender would be a published one, so every deployment that forgot to configure
# it would send escalation mail from the same domain — which is how a domain
# earns a reputation it did not want and how one property's notifications start
# landing in another's spam folder. Display name is allowed.
# EMAIL_FROM=Concierge <desk@yourproperty.com>
#
# EMAIL_API_KEY is required outside APP_ENV=local. Unset in local means the
# provider records what it would have sent and sends nothing, and says so loudly
# at boot. Plain fetch, no SDK: a transactional-email API is one POST.
# EMAIL_API_KEY=re_...
#
# EMAIL_ESCALATION_FALLBACK is required outside APP_ENV=local: where a request
# goes when the property configured no `escalation.contacts` of channel `email`.
# There is deliberately NO hardcoded default — an address in the repository would
# collect every tenant's escalations forever, whether or not anyone still reads
# it. Because it is deployment-wide rather than tenant-scoped, mail sent to it
# omits the guest's message and carries the request id plus a console link.
# EMAIL_ESCALATION_FALLBACK=oncall@youroperator.com
#
# CONSOLE_BASE_URL is the absolute base of the staff console, for the link in the
# body. Defaults to http://localhost:3000 in local; set it anywhere real, or the
# link points at a machine the recipient is not sitting at.
# CONSOLE_BASE_URL=https://console.youroperator.com

# --- W00-13: pipeline plumbing (CI-only, never read by the app) -------------
# `npm run secrets:sync -- --env-file=.env.staging --env=staging` places whatever is
# in the file into the store the manifest names, so these do not need to be exported
# by hand. They are repo-level because one Vercel project serves every environment.
# VERCEL_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_ORG_ID=
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=
# The origin `smoke` probes after a deploy (a GitHub Environment *variable*).
# API_URL=https://api.staging.example.com

# --- W00-13: the environment contract ---------------------------------------
# `npm run check:readiness -- --env <env> [--file .env.<env>]` decides what is
# required where, and `src/config/manifest.ts` is the single source of truth. The
# short version:
#
#   local    — required: DATABASE_URL, LLM_PRIMARY_MODEL, LLM_FALLBACK_MODEL,
#              EMAIL_FROM. Everything else may be missing: the surface that needs
#              it answers 503 with a reason, which is what a developer's shell is
#              allowed to do. Missing values are reported as warnings, never errors.
#   staging  — required: all of the above plus NEON_AUTH_JWKS_URL (with an issuer),
#              GUEST_COOKIE_SECRET, GUEST_PUBLIC_URL, LLM_API_KEY, EMBEDDING_API_KEY,
#              TURNSTILE_SECRET, NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY, EMAIL_API_KEY,
#              EMAIL_ESCALATION_FALLBACK, CONSOLE_BASE_URL, REVIEW_API_URL.
#   prod     — identical to staging. Production is never asked for something a
#              rehearsal was not, because a deploy that passes every rehearsal and
#              fails on the night is the failure this rule exists to prevent.
#
# staging and prod refuse to boot half-configured (`resolveBootMode`). A host that
# genuinely needs to run degraded — a Vercel preview with no secrets — says so
# explicitly, and only staging may:
#
# ALLOW_DEGRADED_BOOT=1     # staging only; REFUSED when APP_ENV=prod
